sers, grants and the loopback IdP are synthetic and approve nothing. Disclosed: corrective_case stays audited and unmeasured (ADR-0064 A4.5, D-264); A4.9 residuals 1 and 4 not exercised. screen_api corrected per D-250 O6: reports are in the DO-D/DO-E handback evidence and QA-C's test-evidence/DG7/qa/. QA-C's independent re-run is in progress at this pass. DG7 analyst pass 7 (T-DG7-AN-P7G, base 5458273, 2026-11-16; F-DG7-921): correction of the evidence for 'records, jobs and approvals intact after a full restart'. The DO-D8 and DO-D8B handbacks found (DO-D8 §4, DO-D8B §4) that before DO-D8 the drill's stop of PostgreSQL sent SIGTERM only to pg_start's launcher subshell, so the D9 runs of DO-D7 and DO-E (and QA round-2 A18) most probably did not restart PostgreSQL itself: only the API and worker restarts were real. Their work trees are gone, so this is an inference from the measured mechanism, not a proof either way; those runs are kept above as history and are not the evidence for the full-restart clause. The evidence for that clause is DO-D8B's T-B11 run (docs/delivery/handbacks/DG7/T-DG7-DO-D8B-evidence/t-b11-run.log; driver exit 0, D0-D11 PASS): its D9 step runs 'stop worker; ... stop API, PostgreSQL; start PostgreSQL, API, worker; job-check compare; ...', job-check reports 'PASS: enabled schedules equal, earlier job_run rows unchanged (0 new), approval rows equal', the export queued before the restart completes after it with equal SHA-256, 2 evidence downloads have equal SHA-256, reconcile after use matches, and D9 PASS in 9.573 s. In that drill code (deploy/scripts/transfer-drill.sh, D9: td_stop_pg on the postmaster and td_stop_pg_dir on pgT/data, else 'D9: the target PostgreSQL did not stop before its restart' and FAIL) a PASS requires the old postmaster to have stopped; that line is absent from the log. The log itself prints no postmaster pid or shutdown line, so the real stop rests on that code path plus the PASS. The drill code is unchanged from DO-D8B's base 7c819f08c to this base (git diff empty). DO-D8's own T-B11 (T-DG7-DO-D8-evidence/t-b11-run.log) also shows D9 PASS after the fix, but its driver ended with exit 2 (cleanup, F-DG7-963), so it is not cited as the primary run. Acceptance still met; status unchanged. DG7 analyst pass 8 (T-DG7-AN-P7H, base 2f9b873, 2026-11-16; F-DG7-971, D-278, ADR-0064 Amendment 5): Residual disclosed. On candidate 07af9fba, QA round 5 showed the shipped drill and the A18 harness FAIL at D9 whenever a scheduled starter runs on the target between D7 and D9: approval.escalation_scan ('*/15 * * * *' Asia/Riyadh), or a daily starter between 00:05 and 00:45 Asia/Riyadh. Mode 2 classed the starter's execution as unexplained (ADR-0064 A3.9 residual 1). This happened in 2 of QA's 5 D9 runs, about 5-7 % of runs on the clock alone (docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG7/qa/round-5/a18/d9-schedule-window-analysis.log, qa-r5-d9-boundary-repro.sh). The product behaved correctly; the A18 result depended on the wall clock. Rule (D-278, recorded and specified in ADR-0064 Amendment 5 A5.2): D9 still runs the unchanged mode 2 recheck, and reconcile-d9.json keeps its real result. The drill then explains a post-restart write only if it is a schedule-triggered automation_execution with no initiating user (run_as service, trigger kind schedule), whose trigger_key '<code>:YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM' names an enabled job_schedule row on the target and a slot of that row's cron in that row's timezone inside the drill's D7-D9 window ((W0 - 60 s, W1], both bounds read from the target's clock_timestamp()), that was created inside the window, has its own worker audit event and is the only execution for its slot, together with that execution's action results and audit events. Every other added or changed row stays unexplained, and the rule does not apply when the recheck reports any other table or kind (Gate 0). The drill report lists every execution explained this way (D9 entry recheck = matched | schedule_explained | not_matche